      "availability": "relevant_visible",
      "assessment": "MG53 genotype differences in corneal nerve density are visible: tPA-MG53 mice had higher nerve density than WT at days 1, 3 and 7, while mg53-/- mice had lower density than WT at days 3 and 7. The day-2 genotype comparison also found differences in CD206+ cells alongside differences in nerve appearance. In the systemic modRNA comparison, MG53 treatment improved injury-area and clinical readouts and increased day-10 nerve area. These observations are consistent with the recorded neuro-immune account, but they do not establish that it is the only explanation for the nerve differences.",
      "scope": "Mouse central corneal alkali injury; global MG53 genotype comparisons and systemic MG53-modRNA versus GFP-modRNA treatment, at the reported post-injury times.",
      "limitations": [
        "The genotype nerve comparisons do not provide corresponding epithelial-wound trajectories or matched measures of injury severity.",
        "The modRNA comparison reports improved injury-area and clinical outcomes as well as a day-10 nerve outcome, but does not establish whether improved nerve area is independent of improved epithelial healing or reduced injury burden.",
        "CD206 and nerve appearance were measured together at day 2, which does not establish that the macrophage change mediates the nerve outcome.",
        "PM43I reduced nerve regeneration in both genotypes, but the tPA-MG53 advantage remained under inhibition; this supports a contribution from STAT6-associated signaling without identifying the source of the residual difference.",
        "The reported nerve-area measurements and sample sizes do not by themselves establish a direct effect on sensory neurons."
      ],
      "rationale": "The recorded neuro-immune route is compatible with the genotype-associated CD206 and nerve differences and with the PM43I results. A further plausible explanation is that MG53-associated improvements in epithelial closure or overall injury burden reduce secondary nerve damage. The modRNA results show both improved injury-area readouts and increased nerve area, and macrophage depletion produced both delayed epithelial healing and reduced nerve density; neither comparison separates these outcomes causally."
    }
  ],
  "findings": [
    {
      "kind": "plausible_alternative_explanation",
      "target_ids": ["n01", "n12"],
      "description": "MG53-associated nerve preservation or regrowth may be mediated partly by faster epithelial wound closure or lower ongoing corneal injury burden, thereby reducing secondary nerve damage. This is an alternative or additional explanation to a wholly macrophage-mediated neuro-immune route, not a demonstrated mechanism. The modRNA comparison reports reduced injury area and increased day-10 nerve area, while macrophage depletion is associated with both delayed healing and reduced day-3 nerve density; these observations do not distinguish mediation from parallel effects.",
      "basis_ids": ["o08", "o19", "o24", "o25"]
    }
  ],
