      "assessment": "The visible results are consistent with the recorded viscoelastic-rebound explanation, but they do not uniquely establish it. Response/recovery times increase from 350/340 ms at 5.5 kPa to 500/500 ms at 10.5 kPa and 540/550 ms at 15 kPa (o09). The separate cyclic-load observation reports increasing peak resistance responses with pressure (o10), so pressure-dependent changes in the electrical contact/conduction response are another plausible contributor to the measured timing.",
      "scope": "The assessment concerns the reported pressure-dependent timing on one assembled sensor at 5.5, 10.5, and 15 kPa; the number of devices and repeated runs is not stated.",
      "limitations": [
        "The timing criterion and time-resolved mechanical recovery are not provided.",
        "The reported timing values are single values per pressure, with no uncertainty or replicate information.",
        "The cyclic-load observation reports peak response and hold stability, not the transient electrical or mechanical process that determines recovery time."
      ],
      "rationale": "The timing trend is directly observed, and the pressure-dependent peak response makes a pressure-dependent electrical contact or conduction transient plausible in this layered sensor. Neither observation separates that possibility from slower viscoelastic rebound. The 0.5–3.0 Hz result addresses signal attenuation under cyclic loading, not the cause of pressure-dependent response or recovery times."
    }
  ],
  "findings": [
    {
      "kind": "plausible_alternative",
      "target_ids": ["n06"],
      "description": "Pressure-dependent evolution of conductive contacts between the coated sponge and micro-protruded film could contribute to the measured resistance response and recovery times, independently of—or in addition to—slower bulk viscoelastic rebound. This is a hypothesis consistent with the reported timing trend and increasing peak response, not a mechanism demonstrated by the visible observations.",
      "basis_ids": ["o09", "o10"]
    }
